1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,114
Take the first. A pull request is GitHub's
proposal to merge one branch into

2
00:00:09,154 --> 00:00:12,972
another, and red means that one
of its automated checks failed.

3
00:00:13,012 --> 00:00:17,687
The textbook's answer: almost always
because the job didn't run what you ran.

4
00:00:17,727 --> 00:00:22,709
Another commit, another shell, another tool
version, no secrets, an empty history.

5
00:00:22,749 --> 00:00:27,730
Each cause is a documented default. And
then the sentence that separates two kinds

6
00:00:27,770 --> 00:00:31,833
of engineer. One who knows the
defaults finds the cause in minutes.

7
00:00:31,873 --> 00:00:34,405
One who doesn't re-runs the job and hopes.

8
00:00:34,745 --> 00:00:39,433
Re-running and hoping has a recognizable
first move: open the red step and start

9
00:00:39,473 --> 00:00:44,339
reading the log. This video gives you a
different first move, and eleven more after

10
00:00:44,379 --> 00:00:47,353
it. The log is step nine.
Why ninth, and not first?

11
00:00:47,393 --> 00:00:49,776
Keep that question for the demonstration.

12
00:00:51,616 --> 00:00:55,549
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:55,589 --> 00:00:59,848
This video opens the last module
of Part 6: runners and debugging.

14
00:00:59,888 --> 00:01:04,798
A runner is the machine that runs one
job, and a job is a list of steps in a

15
00:01:04,838 --> 00:01:08,511
workflow, the file that tells
GitHub Actions what to run.

16
00:01:08,551 --> 00:01:13,722
From here to the gate, the subject is the
skill that Chapter 20B says a CTO pays

17
00:01:13,762 --> 00:01:18,086
for: finding out why a run failed,
in a fixed order, with evidence.

18
00:01:18,426 --> 00:01:21,967
Four topics, in the order of
sections 20B.8 to 20B.11.

19
00:01:22,007 --> 00:01:27,404
GitHub-hosted runners: what they are and
why a label ending in -latest is a moving

20
00:01:27,444 --> 00:01:31,978
target. Self-hosted runners: the
risks, and what "ephemeral" changes.

21
00:01:32,018 --> 00:01:37,283
Limits and billing, as the section gives
them. And the investigation order for a

22
00:01:37,323 --> 00:01:42,852
failing workflow: twelve questions, from
"did the right thing start at all" to "what

23
00:01:42,892 --> 00:01:43,648
did it say".

24
00:01:43,988 --> 00:01:48,986
A note on the numbers. Limits and prices
in this video are quoted from section

25
00:01:49,026 --> 00:01:54,347
20B.10 and are re-verified on the recording
day. If you watch this later, check the

26
00:01:54,387 --> 00:01:58,997
price page before you write a cost
estimate. The textbook says the same.

27
00:02:00,837 --> 00:02:02,416
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:02:02,756 --> 00:02:04,168
After this video you can:

29
00:02:04,508 --> 00:02:09,351
say what a GitHub-hosted runner is and
why a -latest label is a moving target;

30
00:02:09,691 --> 00:02:13,332
state the risks of self-hosted
runners and what "ephemeral" changes;

31
00:02:13,672 --> 00:02:17,149
state the limits and the billing
model as the section gives them;

32
00:02:17,489 --> 00:02:23,203
recite the investigation order: workflow,
event, permissions, runner, environment,

33
00:02:23,243 --> 00:02:28,536
dependencies, secrets, action versions,
logs, artifacts, cache, concurrency;

34
00:02:28,876 --> 00:02:31,910
explain why the log is not
the first thing to read.

35
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:38,809
Hosted runners. In one sentence: a GitHub-hosted
runner is a fresh virtual machine,

36
00:02:38,849 --> 00:02:43,110
built from a published image, that
runs one job and is then destroyed.

37
00:02:43,450 --> 00:02:48,381
runs-on selects it by label. A virtual
machine is a computer made in software, and

38
00:02:48,421 --> 00:02:50,927
the image is the template it's built from.

39
00:02:51,267 --> 00:02:58,405
ubuntu-latest is Ubuntu 24.04 on the first
of October 2026, and GitHub has announced

40
00:02:58,445 --> 00:03:05,412
its migration to Ubuntu 26.04 between the
nineteenth of October and the nineteenth

41
00:03:05,452 --> 00:03:10,197
of November 2026. windows-latest
is Windows Server 2025.

42
00:03:10,237 --> 00:03:16,776
macos-latest is macOS 26 on arm64, moved
from macOS 15 in June and July 2026.

43
00:03:16,816 --> 00:03:20,536
Then the fixed labels,
which name a version.

44
00:03:20,876 --> 00:03:26,886
ubuntu-22.04 is deprecated since the
seventeenth of September 2026, with the end of

45
00:03:26,926 --> 00:03:30,676
support announced for the
seventeenth of April 2027.

46
00:03:30,716 --> 00:03:35,998
For macos-14 the announced end of
support is the second of November 2026.

47
00:03:36,038 --> 00:03:42,048
Those three dates lie after the course
baseline: check the changelog on the day you

48
00:03:42,088 --> 00:03:42,849
watch this.

49
00:03:43,189 --> 00:03:48,429
And ubuntu-slim: one CPU, runs the job
in a container, with jobs limited to 15

50
00:03:48,469 --> 00:03:48,971
minutes.

51
00:03:49,311 --> 00:03:54,575
The textbook's comparison is one you can
keep: a -latest label is a moving name, the

52
00:03:54,615 --> 00:03:59,691
runner equivalent of a branch. A migration
is rolled out gradually over weeks, so

53
00:03:59,731 --> 00:04:03,859
two runs of one workflow on the
same day can get different images.

54
00:04:04,199 --> 00:04:09,823
And a fixed label isn't frozen either.
Images are rebuilt weekly, so tool versions

55
00:04:09,863 --> 00:04:15,556
inside a fixed label move too. The textbook's
example: in May 2026 Node 20 left the

56
00:04:15,596 --> 00:04:21,220
images and the default node became 22.
That's why the course workflows use a fixed

57
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:26,263
label and also install their tools
with setup actions at stated versions.

58
00:04:26,603 --> 00:04:30,959
The cost of pinning: you must move the
label yourself before the image retires.

59
00:04:30,999 --> 00:04:33,296
Put the retirement dates in your calendar.

60
00:04:33,636 --> 00:04:38,968
Where do you see which image a job received?
The "Set up job" section of a run's log

61
00:04:39,008 --> 00:04:44,341
names it. The textbook adds a caveat that
I pass on: the Phase 0 notes didn't verify

62
00:04:44,381 --> 00:04:47,667
that section separately, so
read it in your own run.

63
00:04:48,007 --> 00:04:54,042
Hardware. Standard Linux and Windows runners
have 4 CPUs and 16 gigabytes of memory

64
00:04:54,082 --> 00:04:59,532
in public repositories, and 2 CPUs and
8 gigabytes in private ones, with 14

65
00:04:59,572 --> 00:05:05,023
gigabytes of disk in both. macOS arm64
runners have 3 CPUs and 7 gigabytes.

66
00:05:05,063 --> 00:05:11,171
So a test suite that fits in a public
repository can run out of memory or time after

67
00:05:11,211 --> 00:05:13,441
the repository is made private.

68
00:05:13,781 --> 00:05:18,416
Self-hosted runners. In one sentence: a
self-hosted runner is the same runner

69
00:05:18,456 --> 00:05:23,212
program on a machine you operate, which
asks GitHub for jobs and runs them with

70
00:05:23,252 --> 00:05:25,215
whatever access that machine has.

71
00:05:25,555 --> 00:05:30,261
The risk, in GitHub's own words from the
secure use reference: self-hosted runners

72
00:05:30,301 --> 00:05:34,892
"should almost never be used for public
repositories", because anyone can open a

73
00:05:34,932 --> 00:05:39,522
pull request that runs code on them. The
warning extends to private repositories

74
00:05:39,562 --> 00:05:43,169
where anyone with read access can
fork and open a pull request.

75
00:05:43,509 --> 00:05:48,527
A hosted runner is a clean machine per job.
A persistent self-hosted runner "can be

76
00:05:48,567 --> 00:05:52,063
persistently compromised by
untrusted code in a workflow".

77
00:05:52,103 --> 00:05:57,061
And on a self-hosted runner an environment
doesn't isolate secrets from other jobs

78
00:05:57,101 --> 00:05:58,280
on the same machine.

79
00:05:58,620 --> 00:06:03,661
Ephemeral runners. Registering with the
--ephemeral option gives a runner that takes

80
00:06:03,701 --> 00:06:08,681
one job and is removed. So-called just-in-time
runners are created through the REST

81
00:06:08,721 --> 00:06:13,399
API and also run at most one job. GitHub
recommends autoscaling with ephemeral

82
00:06:13,439 --> 00:06:16,786
runners and advises against
autoscaling persistent ones.

83
00:06:16,826 --> 00:06:21,806
And one more sentence from the documentation:
reusing hardware for such runners can

84
00:06:21,846 --> 00:06:26,766
still expose information from the environment.
The textbook's reading: "ephemeral"

85
00:06:26,806 --> 00:06:28,097
must include the disk.

86
00:06:28,437 --> 00:06:32,939
Runner groups restrict which repositories
may send jobs to which runners.

87
00:06:32,979 --> 00:06:37,979
They're available to organizations on every
plan since the seventeenth of October

88
00:06:38,019 --> 00:06:42,770
2024. The risk is a group open to "all
repositories": every repository in the

89
00:06:42,810 --> 00:06:47,810
organization, including the least reviewed
one, can run code on the machines that

90
00:06:47,850 --> 00:06:49,117
can reach production.

91
00:06:49,457 --> 00:06:55,896
Versions. Since the twenty-ninth of September
2026 a runner older than 2.329.0 can't

92
00:06:55,936 --> 00:07:02,375
register on github.com, and a runner must
install each new release within 30 days or

93
00:07:02,415 --> 00:07:08,545
it stops receiving jobs. That bites fleets
built from a fixed image with updates

94
00:07:08,585 --> 00:07:09,239
disabled.

95
00:07:09,579 --> 00:07:15,127
Queueing. A job waits until a matching
runner is online and fails after 24 hours in

96
00:07:15,167 --> 00:07:18,965
the queue. A self-hosted job
may run for up to five days.

97
00:07:19,305 --> 00:07:19,864
Limits.

98
00:07:20,204 --> 00:07:26,043
A job on a GitHub-hosted runner: 6 hours.
A workflow run, including waiting for

99
00:07:26,083 --> 00:07:30,805
approval: 35 days. An environment
approval: fails after 30 days.

100
00:07:30,845 --> 00:07:36,683
A matrix: 256 jobs per run. Re-runs: 50
per workflow run, within 30 days of the

101
00:07:36,723 --> 00:07:42,487
first run. Reusable workflows: 10 levels,
50 unique called workflows per file.

102
00:07:42,827 --> 00:07:48,478
Concurrent jobs on standard runners: 20
on Free, 40 on Pro, 60 on Team, 500 on

103
00:07:48,518 --> 00:07:54,460
Enterprise. For macOS, 5, or 50 on Enterprise.
Cache: ten gigabytes per repository

104
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:58,764
without charge, with entries
unused for seven days evicted.

105
00:07:58,804 --> 00:08:04,892
Artifacts and logs: 90 days by default, and
since the first of October 2026 the same

106
00:08:04,932 --> 00:08:08,321
setting also removes
checks, runs and statuses.

107
00:08:08,661 --> 00:08:12,941
Billing. Standard hosted runners
are free in public repositories.

108
00:08:12,981 --> 00:08:18,392
Self-hosted runners are free. Private
repositories include 2,000 minutes per month

109
00:08:18,432 --> 00:08:23,510
on Free, 3,000 on Pro and Team, and
50,000 on Enterprise Cloud, with artifact

110
00:08:23,550 --> 00:08:27,233
storage allowances that are
shared with GitHub Packages.

111
00:08:27,573 --> 00:08:33,919
List prices per minute since the first of
January 2026: Linux 2-core, 0.6 cents.

112
00:08:33,959 --> 00:08:38,229
Linux arm64 2-core, 0.5 cents.
ubuntu-slim, 0.2 cents.

113
00:08:38,269 --> 00:08:44,855
Windows 2-core, 1 cent. macOS, 6.2 cents.
Each job is rounded up to a whole minute.

114
00:08:44,895 --> 00:08:51,480
Storage beyond the allowance: artifacts 25
cents and cache 7 cents per gigabyte per

115
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:56,909
month. Larger runners are always
billed, in public repositories too.

116
00:08:56,949 --> 00:09:03,055
A reusable workflow is billed to the
caller. Time spent in a wait timer isn't

117
00:09:03,095 --> 00:09:03,614
billed.

118
00:09:03,954 --> 00:09:09,186
One thing here is unverified, and it's
on screen. How Windows and macOS minutes

119
00:09:09,226 --> 00:09:14,791
consume the included minutes isn't stated
on any 2026 page the research could fetch.

120
00:09:14,831 --> 00:09:19,462
Don't quote a multiplier. Read your
own usage in the billing settings.

121
00:09:19,802 --> 00:09:25,271
And a version note. Prices were cut by
up to 39 percent on the first of January

122
00:09:25,311 --> 00:09:31,059
2026, so older tutorials quote more. One
charge is announced and not in effect: 0.2

123
00:09:31,099 --> 00:09:36,010
cents per minute for self-hosted
runners, postponed without a new date.

124
00:09:36,350 --> 00:09:39,936
Quick quiz, on that rounding
rule. Which costs more?

125
00:09:39,976 --> 00:09:45,724
A, one job with ten steps of six seconds
each. B, ten jobs with one six-second step

126
00:09:45,764 --> 00:09:46,979
each. Your answer?

127
00:09:51,819 --> 00:09:56,991
B. Each job is rounded up to a whole
minute, so ten short jobs are billed as ten

128
00:09:57,031 --> 00:10:02,398
minutes, and the one job as one. Almost
everyone misses that once, because the work

129
00:10:02,438 --> 00:10:03,180
is the same.

130
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:09,307
The cost levers follow from the rules.
Cancel superseded CI runs with a concurrency

131
00:10:09,347 --> 00:10:15,063
group. Prefer one job with several steps
over many one-step jobs, because each job

132
00:10:15,103 --> 00:10:20,749
rounds up. Keep macOS for what needs macOS,
at roughly ten times the Linux price.

133
00:10:20,789 --> 00:10:24,821
Use ubuntu-slim for glue jobs.
Shorten artifact retention.

134
00:10:24,861 --> 00:10:29,805
Path filters also save minutes, and
video 154 shows what they cost you.

135
00:10:30,145 --> 00:10:34,861
The investigation order. The textbook's
instruction: a failing run tempts you to

136
00:10:34,901 --> 00:10:38,428
open the red step and start
reading. Do that last but three.

137
00:10:38,468 --> 00:10:43,421
The order goes from "did the right thing
start at all" to "what did it say", because

138
00:10:43,461 --> 00:10:47,286
an answer early in the list makes
everything after it irrelevant.

139
00:10:47,626 --> 00:10:52,475
One. Workflow. Which workflow file,
from which commit, defined this run?

140
00:10:52,815 --> 00:10:57,576
Two. Event. What triggered it, and which
ref and commit did the job check out?

141
00:10:57,616 --> 00:11:01,823
push builds the pushed commit.
pull_request builds refs/pull/N/merge.

142
00:11:01,863 --> 00:11:04,532
A re-run reuses the
original commit and ref.

143
00:11:04,872 --> 00:11:10,118
Three. Permissions. What could the token
do? Top-level and job-level permissions.

144
00:11:10,158 --> 00:11:14,883
Unlisted scopes are none. Fork and
Dependabot runs get a read-only token.

145
00:11:15,223 --> 00:11:20,633
Four. Runner. Which image, which size? The
label, the image named at the top of the

146
00:11:20,673 --> 00:11:24,377
job log, and whether the
repository is public or private.

147
00:11:24,717 --> 00:11:29,324
Five. Environment. Did the job
reference one, and did its rules pass?

148
00:11:29,364 --> 00:11:34,106
Waiting, rejected, wrong branch, or
an environment created by accident.

149
00:11:34,446 --> 00:11:38,862
Six. Dependencies. Were the same
versions installed as locally?

150
00:11:38,902 --> 00:11:43,176
The lock file, --locked, the
versions printed by setup steps.

151
00:11:43,516 --> 00:11:49,233
Seven. Secrets. Were they present? An unset
or withheld secret is an empty string,

152
00:11:49,273 --> 00:11:50,146
not an error.

153
00:11:50,486 --> 00:11:54,687
Eight. Action versions. Which
commit of each action ran?

154
00:11:54,727 --> 00:11:58,247
The pins, an old major
on Node 24, a moved tag.

155
00:11:58,587 --> 00:12:01,866
Nine. Logs. What did
the failed step print?

156
00:12:02,206 --> 00:12:06,719
Ten. Artifacts. Were the expected
files produced and passed on?

157
00:12:07,059 --> 00:12:10,397
Eleven. Cache. What was
restored, under which key?

158
00:12:10,737 --> 00:12:15,318
Twelve. Concurrency. Was the run
cancelled or replaced by another?

159
00:12:15,658 --> 00:12:19,095
All of these are 🟢 SAFE: they
change nothing on GitHub.

160
00:12:19,135 --> 00:12:22,571
gh run download writes files
into your local directory.

161
00:12:22,911 --> 00:12:27,755
The textbook's summary of the method: steps
1 and 2 remove the most confusion for

162
00:12:27,795 --> 00:12:32,398
the least effort. gh run view prints the
event, the branch and the commit ID.

163
00:12:32,438 --> 00:12:37,403
Compare that ID with git rev-parse HEAD on
your machine before you compare anything

164
00:12:37,443 --> 00:12:37,705
else.

165
00:12:39,545 --> 00:12:40,518
A picture helps.

166
00:12:40,858 --> 00:12:43,828
Analogy for self-hosted
runners, from the textbook.

167
00:12:43,868 --> 00:12:47,074
Lending your workshop to
anyone who holds a work order.

168
00:12:47,114 --> 00:12:51,678
A GitHub-hosted runner is a rented workshop
that is demolished after each job.

169
00:12:51,718 --> 00:12:56,105
Yours stays: tools, leftovers, and
everything a previous visitor hid there.

170
00:12:56,445 --> 00:13:01,254
The analogy breaks in one respect, and the
textbook says that respect is everything:

171
00:13:01,294 --> 00:13:05,584
you choose who may write work orders,
and that choice is the whole security

172
00:13:05,624 --> 00:13:06,103
question.

173
00:13:06,443 --> 00:13:11,530
A model for the investigation order. Think
of a run as a chain of decisions, each

174
00:13:11,570 --> 00:13:15,833
made before the next can matter.
Was a run created, from which file?

175
00:13:15,873 --> 00:13:19,314
For which commit? With what
authority? On what machine?

176
00:13:19,354 --> 00:13:24,060
Past which gate? With which dependencies,
which secrets, which action code?

177
00:13:24,100 --> 00:13:27,415
Only then does your command
start and print anything.

178
00:13:27,755 --> 00:13:32,401
The log is the output of the last link. If
an earlier link was different from what

179
00:13:32,441 --> 00:13:36,116
you assume, the log is a faithful
record of the wrong experiment.

180
00:13:36,156 --> 00:13:40,802
You can read it for an hour and learn
nothing, because nothing in it says "this is

181
00:13:40,842 --> 00:13:42,288
not the commit you think".

182
00:13:42,628 --> 00:13:46,949
That's why the order is fixed and why
the log sits at nine, which answers the

183
00:13:46,989 --> 00:13:51,083
question from the opening. The three
steps after it, artifacts, cache and

184
00:13:51,123 --> 00:13:54,707
concurrency, are about what the
run handed on or was done to it.

185
00:13:56,547 --> 00:14:01,832
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds. Look
away from the screen and write the first

186
00:14:01,872 --> 00:14:04,462
four words of the order. Then check them.

187
00:14:06,802 --> 00:14:11,483
Workflow, event, permissions, runner. If
you had the first two, you have the two

188
00:14:11,523 --> 00:14:14,552
that remove the most confusion
for the least effort.

189
00:14:16,392 --> 00:14:17,152
Into the lab.

190
00:14:17,492 --> 00:14:20,853
The developer's report: "The
checks pass on my machine.

191
00:14:20,893 --> 00:14:22,399
The pull request is red."

192
00:14:22,739 --> 00:14:24,886
Step 1: what the developer sees.

193
00:14:25,226 --> 00:14:28,273
On the branch feature/bulk-reorder,
both checks pass.

194
00:14:28,313 --> 00:14:30,428
The developer isn't wrong about that.

195
00:14:30,768 --> 00:14:35,316
Step 2 of the order, before any log:
which commit did the job check out?

196
00:14:35,356 --> 00:14:39,586
For a pull_request run you know the
answer in kind: the test merge.

197
00:14:39,626 --> 00:14:42,071
So look at the two sides of that merge.

198
00:14:42,411 --> 00:14:46,047
What do you expect to see on main
that the branch doesn't have?

199
00:14:46,087 --> 00:14:46,980
Say it out loud.

200
00:14:56,820 --> 00:15:03,193
main has a commit by Asha, 2230054, "Require
a safety stock in the reorder rules".

201
00:15:03,233 --> 00:15:09,606
The branch has 34f82ef, "Add bulk reorder".
They share the merge base 57c8425, the

202
00:15:09,646 --> 00:15:16,019
most recent commit that both histories
contain. Two people changed related code on

203
00:15:16,059 --> 00:15:16,801
two sides.

204
00:15:17,141 --> 00:15:20,539
Step 3: build what the pull
request run checks out.

205
00:15:20,879 --> 00:15:24,019
git merge-tree reads and
writes an unreferenced tree.

206
00:15:24,059 --> 00:15:27,439
The switch detaches HEAD on
main so that no branch moves.

207
00:15:27,479 --> 00:15:31,339
git merge is 🟡 CAUTION, and here
it moves only the detached HEAD.

208
00:15:31,679 --> 00:15:35,962
The merge is clean. Will both checks
pass on it? Make your prediction.

209
00:15:40,802 --> 00:15:44,306
"HEAD (no branch)", as on the
runner. The first check passes.

210
00:15:44,346 --> 00:15:48,954
The second fails with a TypeError: a
function now needs an argument that the new

211
00:15:48,994 --> 00:15:50,000
code doesn't pass.

212
00:15:50,340 --> 00:15:54,517
Asha's commit changed a signature,
and the branch calls the old one.

213
00:15:54,557 --> 00:15:58,053
No conflict, because the changes
are in different places.

214
00:15:58,093 --> 00:16:00,348
The failure exists only in the merge.

215
00:16:00,688 --> 00:16:02,657
Step 4: the wrong reflex.

216
00:16:02,997 --> 00:16:07,735
Back on the branch, the check passes again.
The comment in the transcript is the

217
00:16:07,775 --> 00:16:12,215
lesson: re-checking the branch alone
reproduces nothing, however often it's

218
00:16:12,255 --> 00:16:17,113
repeated. On GitHub the equivalent is
pressing re-run, and you know from video 145

219
00:16:17,153 --> 00:16:19,562
that a re-run reuses the original commit.

220
00:16:19,902 --> 00:16:21,828
Step 5: recovery.

221
00:16:22,168 --> 00:16:26,704
Bring the base into the branch, and the
failure is now on the branch, where you can

222
00:16:26,744 --> 00:16:30,564
work on it. One call is fixed and
committed, and git commit is 🟢 SAFE.

223
00:16:30,904 --> 00:16:35,954
The graph shows the merge 9c7cc7e
and the fix 2cb0b73 on top.

224
00:16:36,294 --> 00:16:39,026
Step 6: verify the same way CI will.

225
00:16:39,366 --> 00:16:44,091
A new test merge on a detached HEAD, both
checks pass, and you return to the branch

226
00:16:44,131 --> 00:16:48,627
with a clean status. You have verified
the thing the next run will test, before

227
00:16:48,667 --> 00:16:49,086
pushing.

228
00:16:49,426 --> 00:16:53,892
Count what you didn't do: you never read
a log. Step 2 of the order answered the

229
00:16:53,932 --> 00:16:54,399
question.

230
00:16:54,739 --> 00:16:58,595
Lab 28.2 on your practice
repository, in your normal shell.

231
00:16:58,635 --> 00:17:04,076
The interface changes. The lab text and the
linked documentation are the reference.

232
00:17:04,116 --> 00:17:07,048
No GitHub output was
captured by the authors.

233
00:17:07,388 --> 00:17:12,306
Take a failing run from the lab and go down
the list with the run page and the CLI

234
00:17:12,346 --> 00:17:17,263
side by side. For step 1 and 2, find on
the run page the workflow name, the event,

235
00:17:17,303 --> 00:17:22,221
the branch and the commit ID, and compare
the commit ID with git rev-parse HEAD in

236
00:17:22,261 --> 00:17:22,886
your clone.

237
00:17:23,226 --> 00:17:28,243
For step 3, read the permissions blocks
of the workflow file at that commit.

238
00:17:28,283 --> 00:17:32,435
For step 4, expand the "Set up
job" section and find the image.

239
00:17:32,475 --> 00:17:36,228
For step 5, look at whether
the job names an environment.

240
00:17:36,268 --> 00:17:39,621
Only when you reach step
9, expand the failed step.

241
00:17:39,661 --> 00:17:42,549
Write down at which step
you had the answer.

242
00:17:44,389 --> 00:17:46,037
Five mistakes to watch for.

243
00:17:46,377 --> 00:17:51,233
Reading the log first. Root cause: most
failures are decided before the first log

244
00:17:51,273 --> 00:17:55,827
line, by the workflow file, the event,
the commit, the token or the machine.

245
00:17:56,167 --> 00:18:00,804
Re-running to reproduce or to "pick up
main". Root cause: a re-run reuses the

246
00:18:00,844 --> 00:18:02,261
original commit and ref.

247
00:18:02,601 --> 00:18:06,406
Using -latest labels and
being surprised by a change.

248
00:18:06,446 --> 00:18:12,498
Root cause: a -latest label is a moving
name, and migrations roll out gradually over

249
00:18:12,538 --> 00:18:12,934
weeks.

250
00:18:13,274 --> 00:18:17,854
A persistent self-hosted runner for a
repository that accepts pull requests from

251
00:18:17,894 --> 00:18:22,589
forks. Root cause: the machine runs whatever
a workflow tells it, and what one job

252
00:18:22,629 --> 00:18:24,668
leaves behind is there for the next.

253
00:18:25,008 --> 00:18:29,820
Many one-step jobs "for clarity". Root
cause: each job is rounded up to a whole

254
00:18:29,860 --> 00:18:31,970
minute and starts on a new machine.

255
00:18:33,810 --> 00:18:38,616
Now, out of the lab. Two cases from
the textbook, both from ML teams.

256
00:18:38,956 --> 00:18:44,224
The first. A model-evaluation job that fits
in the team's public mirror is killed in

257
00:18:44,264 --> 00:18:49,405
the private repository, with identical
code. The textbook's instruction: ask first

258
00:18:49,445 --> 00:18:53,006
for the visibility of the
repository, then for the label.

259
00:18:53,046 --> 00:18:56,798
Public means 4 CPUs and 16
gigabytes. Private means 2 and 8.

260
00:18:56,838 --> 00:19:01,284
That's step 4 of the order, and no
log line will say it in those words.

261
00:19:01,624 --> 00:19:06,780
The second. An ML team runs GPU evaluation
on its own machines, because hosted GPU

262
00:19:06,820 --> 00:19:11,152
minutes are expensive. The textbook
calls this the defensible design.

263
00:19:11,192 --> 00:19:16,284
Ephemeral runners, in a runner group that
only the evaluation repository may use.

264
00:19:16,324 --> 00:19:21,227
Workflows on those runners triggered only
by push to protected branches and by

265
00:19:21,267 --> 00:19:24,522
workflow_dispatch, never by
pull_request from forks.

266
00:19:24,562 --> 00:19:27,753
And no long-lived cloud
credentials on the machine.

267
00:19:29,593 --> 00:19:36,907
Your turn. Do Lab 28.2, "The investigation
order, applied to a failing run", in

268
00:19:36,947 --> 00:19:40,537
lab-manual/m28-runners-debugging-ci.md.

269
00:19:40,877 --> 00:19:44,500
Before you open anything, write
the twelve steps from memory.

270
00:19:44,540 --> 00:19:49,303
Then, for the failing run, predict at
which step the cause will show itself, and

271
00:19:49,343 --> 00:19:51,705
write that number down before you start.

272
00:19:52,045 --> 00:19:58,213
The challenge is Exercise 28.4,
"What does this pipeline cost?", in

273
00:19:58,253 --> 00:20:01,735
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

274
00:20:05,575 --> 00:20:10,233
Answer out loud. A strong answer gives
the order as a list with one question per

275
00:20:10,273 --> 00:20:15,166
step, and then the principle behind it: what
it means that an early answer makes the

276
00:20:15,206 --> 00:20:19,923
later steps irrelevant. It names the two
steps that remove the most confusion for

277
00:20:19,963 --> 00:20:23,153
the least effort and the one
comparison you make first.

278
00:20:23,193 --> 00:20:28,028
The follow-up asks for a concrete case in
which reading the log first costs an hour

279
00:20:28,068 --> 00:20:32,667
that the second step would have saved.
You've replayed one, so tell it with the

280
00:20:32,707 --> 00:20:33,489
commits named.

281
00:20:35,329 --> 00:20:36,256
Let's land this.

282
00:20:36,596 --> 00:20:38,256
You should now be able to say:

283
00:20:38,596 --> 00:20:43,843
A hosted runner is a fresh virtual machine
per job from an image that changes; a

284
00:20:43,883 --> 00:20:45,891
-latest label is a moving name.

285
00:20:46,231 --> 00:20:50,847
A self-hosted runner runs whatever a
workflow tells it with the machine's access,

286
00:20:50,887 --> 00:20:53,778
and persists unless it is
ephemeral, disk included.

287
00:20:54,118 --> 00:20:58,658
Jobs are rounded up to whole minutes;
private repositories get smaller standard

288
00:20:58,698 --> 00:21:01,789
runners; prices and limits
must be checked on the day.

289
00:21:02,129 --> 00:21:07,565
The investigation order is workflow,
event, permissions, runner, environment,

290
00:21:07,605 --> 00:21:12,969
dependencies, secrets, action versions,
logs, artifacts, cache, concurrency.

291
00:21:13,309 --> 00:21:18,219
The log is ninth because everything above
it was decided before the first log line.

292
00:21:20,059 --> 00:21:27,999
Read sections 20B.8 to 20B.11 of Chapter
20B. Do Exercise 28.1, "Where in the

293
00:21:28,039 --> 00:21:33,181
order?", in
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

294
00:21:33,521 --> 00:21:37,833
You now have a first move that isn't
the log, and eleven more after it.

295
00:21:37,873 --> 00:21:42,858
Write the twelve words from memory once
today. Next time: passes locally, fails on

296
00:21:42,898 --> 00:21:47,884
GitHub Actions, and the documented causes.
Until then, look at the state first and

297
00:21:47,924 --> 00:21:50,151
type second. See you in the next one.
